  "race": "tiefling",
  "class": "barbarian",
  "background": "folk_hero",
  "backstory": "Born under a blood-red moon in the craggy foothills of the Spine, the child was never given a name by the terrified villagers who found him abandoned on their doorstep. Instead, he grew up in the shadows of the communal forge, a silent, hulking youth with skin like cooling embers and horns that swept back like a mountain ram’s. His life was one of quiet isolation until the night the Frost-Giants descended upon the valley. While the local guard fled or fell, the nameless tiefling did not retreat. He did not reach for a polished blade or a scholar’s spell; instead, he tapped into a wellspring of ancient, volcanic fury buried deep within his infernal blood. He seized a heavy, rusted logging axe and held the narrow mountain pass alone for three days and three nights. When the sun rose on the fourth day, the giants were broken, and the villagers found him standing amidst the carnage, breathing steam into the cold air—a savior they still did not know how to address.\n\nThis victory did not bring him a seat at the high table, but it earned him a debt of silence and a legacy of whispered gratitude. He realized then that the rage boiling in his veins was not a curse of his devilish ancestry, but a primal shield for those who could not defend themselves. He rejected the civilized world’s need for titles and registries, choosing instead the path of the wild, where the only truth is the strength of one's grip and the heat of one's heart. He wanders now as a nameless legend of the hinterlands, a folk hero to the downtrodden who share stories of a crimson giant who appears when hope is lost, only to vanish before the thanks can be spoken. \n\nHis journey is driven by a restless search for a purpose greater than mere survival and a desire to define himself by his deeds rather than his lineage. He carries an empty iron locket—a symbol of the identity he never had—and seeks to prove that a man’s worth is forged in the fire of his character. Whether facing down a corrupt baron or a marauding beast, he enters the fray with a roar that echoes like the shifting of tectonic plates. He moves toward the horizon not to find his name, but to ensure that those who live in fear might one day have the luxury of forgetting his, knowing only that they were saved by a force of nature that refused to break.",
  "alignment": "chaotic_neutral",
  "name": "soul",
